course: everyday
lesson: 482
title: Mum's best friend and Dad's mate from work
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every Friday Lily's mum's best friend, Auntie Jo, comes round with a bag of gossip, and the two of them laugh so loudly in the kitchen that Lily hears it through her pillow. «Jo, you're a nightmare,» says Mum. «Yeah, but I'm your nightmare.» Then they fall out over something said at a birthday party, Jo stops coming, and the kitchen goes very quiet. Mum keeps checking her phone. After a month Jo turns up with a cake that says «SORRY» in wonky icing. Mum laughs and cries. Grown-ups have best friends, fall out and make up, just like kids.
explain: Dad's mates come in all shapes. Big Steve from work watches the football, calls Sam «the gaffer» (the boss) and gives him a fiver «for services to the crisps», but swears at the telly so much Mum sends him to the garden at half time. «Good lad, Steve. Bit of a gob on him, mind» (a gob is a mouth; he talks too much and too rudely). Gary moans about everything, the boss, the weather, the referee, but when Dad's car breaks down at midnight, Gary drives forty minutes to fetch him. «Gary's sound» (Northern for reliable and decent). «Likes a whinge, that's all.»
explain: Even old friends must respect your family. At a barbecue Dad's friend Phil makes a nasty joke about Mum's cooking in front of everyone. Dad puts his burger down: «Out of order, Phil. Apologise or go home.» Phil goes home, rings a week later and says sorry to Mum properly. And some friends only take: Connor's mum's friend Sharon only rings to borrow money or a lift, «Hiya hun, quick favour», and never pays back. After the fourth time, Mum says no. Sharon stops ringing. Mum is sad for a day, then fine.
explain: Some friendships last a lifetime. Mia's grandma and Miss Opal from church have known each other sixty years. «Back in my day,» says Miss Opal. «Your day was my day, Opal.» Mum video calls her school friend in Melbourne every Sunday, in her dressing gown. Lily's dad and Pete fish for six hours and say about nine words: «Best day of my week, that.» And when Maddie's mum's friend Donna visits from Newcastle, they're soon talking pure Geordie: «Howay man, divvent be daft, that gadgie was mental» («come on, don't be silly, that bloke was mad»). Maddie, with her new Aussie twang, can't follow a word.
fact: Grown-ups need friends too, and many keep best friends for decades.
fact: Grown-ups fall out and make up as well; it can upset them as much as it upsets children.
fact: A friend can be fun and still have faults, like swearing or moaning; you don't have to copy their faults.
fact: The friend who moans the most may be the one who turns up when you really need help.
fact: Even an old friend should respect your family; standing up to a friend can be the right thing.
fact: A one-way friend only takes, like borrowing and never paying back; it is fine to stop giving.
fact: Friendships can last across the world if both people keep calling.
fact: Some friendships don't need many words; just being together can be enough.
fact: Old friends often bring back the way you talked and acted when you were young.
fact: Friendships last a lifetime when people keep showing up for each other.
fact: «Gaffer» means boss; «sound» means decent and reliable; «hun» is short for honey, a friendly name.
fact: Geordie is the way people talk in and around Newcastle: «howay» means come on, «divvent» means don't, «gadgie» means a man.
exercise: Who comes to Lily's house every Friday? | Mum's best friend, Auntie Jo | Dad's mate Big Steve | Grandma's friend Miss Opal | Connor's mum's friend Sharon
exercise: How did Auntie Jo end the fall-out with Mum? | Brought a cake saying «SORRY» | Sent a long legal letter | Never came back at all | Told Lily to say sorry for her
exercise: Why did Mum send Big Steve to the garden at half time? | He swore at the telly too much | He ate all the crisps | He wanted to smoke | He was cheering for the wrong team
exercise: What does «gaffer» mean? | Boss | Baby | Footballer | Thief
exercise: Gary moans about everything. What did he do when Dad's car broke down? | Drove forty minutes at midnight to get him | Said it was Dad's own fault for having an old car | Turned his phone off | Moaned and stayed home
exercise: Mum's best friend from school lives where? | Melbourne | Newcastle | Galway | Texas
exercise: What did Dad say when Phil insulted Mum's cooking? | «Out of order, Phil. Apologise or go home» | «Ha, good one, Phil» | Nothing, he kept eating | «Mum, say sorry to Phil»
exercise: What did Phil do a week later? | Rang and apologised properly to Mum | Never spoke to the family again | Came round and insulted the cooking all over again | Sent Dad a bill
exercise: How long has Mia's grandma known Miss Opal? | Sixty years | Six months | Six years | Since last Christmas
exercise: What does Sharon usually ring for? | To borrow money or a lift | To say happy birthday | To share good news | To invite them to parties
exercise: What did Connor's mum do after Sharon's fourth unpaid loan? | Said no | Lent her twice as much | Moved house | Paid Sharon's bills too
exercise: How many words did Dad and Pete say on the fishing day? | About nine | About nine hundred | None at all | They talked nonstop
exercise: Why does Dad call fishing with Pete «best day of my week»? | Some friendships don't need words | He catches huge fish every single time | Pete pays him to come along and help | He likes being bored and sitting still
exercise: What happened when Donna visited from Newcastle? | She and Maddie's mum talked pure Geordie | They had a big row about the old days | They spoke only French | They didn't recognise each other at the station
exercise: What does «howay» mean in Geordie? | Come on | Goodbye | Hooray | How are you
test: «Big Steve swears loads so I can too, innit». Right? | No, friends can have faults you don't copy | Yes, if a grown-up does it then it's fine for you | Yes, but only at the football | No, so Steve is a bad person
test: «Gary's always whingin, why's Dad even mates with him». What does the midnight breakdown show? | The moaner may be the one who turns up | Gary only ever moans and never helps anyone | Dad should drop Gary | Moaners are always bad friends to have
test: «Mum and Jo haven't spoken for a month, they're done forever». Is that likely? | Not always, grown-ups make up too | Yes, grown-ups never make up | Yes, after a month it's over | No, they'll make up the very next day
test: Phil cracks a nasty joke about Mum at a barbecue. Is Dad being rude to a friend by speaking up? | No, even old friends must respect your family | Yes, you should never stand up to your friends | Yes, jokes are always fine | No, but he should hit Phil
test: «Hiya hun, quick favour, can I borrow fifty, pay you Friday x». Fourth time, never paid. What's sensible? | Say no, she's a one-way friend | Lend her a hundred this time | Lend it and say nothing | Shout at her in the street
test: «Mum talks to her mate in Australia every Sunday, that's well extra». (extra means over the top.) Why does she? | Friendships last across the world if you keep calling | She has to for her job | She's checking what the weather is like over there | Australia is very close
test: «they sat for six hours and said nine words, that's so dead». What's the truth? | Some friendships don't need words | They must have fallen out on the way there | Fishing is banned from talking by law | They didn't really like each other
test: «Howay man, divvent be daft!» What is Donna saying? | Come on, don't be silly | Go away, you're daft | Hello, how are you | Come here, you're late
test: What would probably happen if Connor's mum kept lending Sharon money? | She'd keep losing money to a one-way friend | Sharon would pay it all back at once | Sharon would turn into a better, kinder friend | Nothing at all would change for either of them
test: In a sitcom, grown-ups seem to have no real friends, just neighbours to argue with. What's true in real life? | Grown-ups need and keep real friends too | Grown-ups don't need friends | Only children ever have real best friends | Grown-up friends never last
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the friendships of adults as described in the lesson? | Adults likewise form, quarrel within and repair friendships | Adults do not require friendships | Adult friendships are invariably free from dispute | Adult friendships terminate upon any quarrel
exam: Identify the principal lesson to be drawn from the conduct of the habitually complaining colleague who assisted at midnight. | A friend's faults do not preclude his reliability in need | Complaining friends are invariably unreliable | Assistance ought to be refused to all who habitually complain | Reliability is proportional to cheerfulness
exam: Ascertain the most appropriate conduct of a person whose acquaintance repeatedly borrows money without repayment. | To decline further loans | To increase the sums lent | To lend in secret from one's family | To pay the acquaintance's other debts
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a friend who disparages one's spouse in company. | It is proper to require an apology notwithstanding the friendship | Friendship requires that such remarks be tolerated without objection | The spouse ought to apologise to the friend | The remark ought to be applauded
